---
import VisaPage from '../../../components/ui/VisaPage.astro';
---
<VisaPage
  lang="en"
  title="Panama Pensionado Visa — Requirements &amp; Benefits"
  description="The Panama Pensionado Visa requires a guaranteed pension of $1,000/month. Permanent residency from day one, no renewals, and legally mandated discounts on healthcare, airfare, hotels and more."
  visaName="Panama Pensionado Visa"
  tag="Retirees with pension"
  mainReq="Lifetime pension of $1,000/month"
  income="$1,000 USD/month (single) · $1,250 combined (couple)"
  processingTime="Approx. 6 months"
  renewalNote="Permanent — no renewals · visit Panama 1 day every 2 years"
  quickAnswer="The Panama Pensionado Visa requires a guaranteed lifetime pension of at least $1,000/month (single applicant) or $1,250/month (couple). It grants permanent residency from the very first application — no renewals ever. Benefits include 25% off international airfare, 50% off entertainment, 20% off medical consultations and surgeries, 10% off prescription medications, and duty-free import of one personal vehicle every two years. Processing takes approximately 6 months. The pension can be from any country — Social Security, military, government, or private company plan — as long as it is guaranteed for life."
  intro="Panama's most popular residency visa and one of the strongest retirement programs in the world. If you receive a guaranteed lifetime pension from any public or private source — government, military, Social Security, or company — you almost certainly qualify. Residency is permanent from day one, never expires, and comes with a legally mandated discount catalog that has no equivalent anywhere in Latin America."
  requirements={[
    "Guaranteed lifetime pension or retirement of at least $1,000 USD/month from any public or private source (government, military, Social Security, company pension) — from any country in the world",
    "For a couple: $1,250/month combined total. For each additional dependent: +$250/month above the applicable threshold",
    "Alternative threshold: $750/month pension minimum if you simultaneously purchase real estate in Panama valued at $100,000 USD or more",
    "Valid passport with at least 6 months of validity remaining, plus complete authenticated copies",
    "National criminal background check from your country of origin — apostilled, maximum 6 months old",
    "Official pension letter or certification from the issuing institution — apostilled and translated by an Authorized Public Translator in Panama",
    "Civil documents: birth certificate (apostilled); marriage certificate if applicable (apostilled)",
    "Medical exam performed in Panama (blood work, chest X-ray, general physical)",
    "Passport-size photos and payment of government fees",
    "All foreign documents must be apostilled or authenticated by the Panama Consulate in your country"
  ]}
  benefits={[
    "Permanent residency from the very first application — no renewals, no expiration",
    "Rights are protected even if Panama's immigration laws change in the future",
    "25% discount on international airfare to any destination",
    "50% discount on entertainment: cinemas, theaters, concerts, cultural events, and sporting events",
    "30–50% discount on hotels and lodging (30% or more Monday–Thursday)",
    "25% discount at full-service restaurants · 15% at fast-food chains",
    "20% discount on prescription medications, medical consultations, and surgeries",
    "30% additional discount on public transportation (buses, trains, boats) Monday–Thursday",
    "One-time duty-free import of household goods and furniture valued up to $10,000 USD",
    "Duty-free import of a new personal vehicle every two years",
    "Option to apply for the Special Panamanian Passport (Documento Especial de Viaje) if eligible — access to 140+ countries without a visa, including the Schengen Area and the United Kingdom",
    "Ability to work in Panama with Servicio Nacional de Migración authorization"
  ]}
  notFor={[
    "You do not have a guaranteed lifetime pension — savings, investments, or remote work income do not qualify for this visa",
    "Your pension is below $1,000/month and you are not prepared to purchase $100,000+ in Panamanian real estate",
    "You want to run active businesses or hold formal employment in Panama under your own name without restrictions — this visa has employment limitations"
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "What types of pension qualify for the Panama Pensionado visa?",

      a: "Any guaranteed lifetime pension from any country qualifies: government pension, Social Security, military benefit, public sector retirement, or private company pension plan. The key requirement is that the income is guaranteed for life — not a savings account, investment dividend, or rental income. The pension can be in any currency, but the equivalent of $1,000 USD/month minimum must be demonstrable through official documentation."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the minimum pension required for the Panama Pensionado visa?",
      a: "The standard requirement is a lifetime pension of at least $1,000/month for a single applicant. For a couple applying together, the combined minimum is $1,250/month. For each additional dependent (beyond a spouse), an additional $250/month is required. There is an alternative pathway: applicants with a pension of at least $750/month may qualify if they simultaneously purchase real estate in Panama valued at $100,000 USD or more."
    },
    {
      q: "Is the Panama Pensionado visa permanent?",
      a: "Yes. The Pensionado visa grants permanent residency from the very first application — no renewals are ever required. The only ongoing requirement is visiting Panama at least once every two years to maintain active status. Rights acquired under this visa are protected even if Panama's immigration laws change in the future."
    },
    {
      q: "What discounts does the Panama Pensionado visa include?",
      a: "The Pensionado visa comes with a legally mandated discount catalog: 25% off international airfare; 50% off cinemas, theaters, concerts, and cultural or sporting events; 30–50% off hotels and lodging; 25% off full-service restaurants, 15% off fast food; 20% off medications, medical consultations, and surgeries; 30% additional discount on public transportation (buses, trains, boats) Monday–Thursday. Additionally, holders receive a one-time duty-free import of household goods and furniture up to $10,000 USD, and duty-free import of a personal vehicle every two years. These discounts apply throughout Panama."
    },
    {
      q: "How long does the Panama Pensionado visa take to process?",
      a: "The process typically takes approximately 6 months from start to finish. Month 1 involves gathering documents and obtaining apostilles in your home country. In Month 2, the applicant travels to Panama for medical exams and formal submission of the application to the Servicio Nacional de Migración. Months 3–5 cover government review, background checks, and inter-ministerial audits. In Month 6, the residency is approved and the holder receives a permanent resident card (carné) and can apply for a cédula. Beyond Panama Relocation does not provide legal services — for your specific process, we connect you with qualified immigration attorneys in Panama."
    },
    {
      q: "Who can be included as a dependent on the Pensionado visa?",
      a: "The following family members may be included as dependents: your spouse; children under 18 years of age; children between 18 and 25 years old who are single, enrolled as full-time students, and economically dependent; children of any age with an accredited disability; and the parents of the main applicant, with no upper age limit. Each dependent requires an additional $250/month of pension income above the applicable base threshold."
    },
    {
      q: "Can I work in Panama with the Pensionado visa?",
      a: "The Pensionado visa does not automatically grant unlimited work authorization. Holders can work in Panama with prior authorization from the Servicio Nacional de Migración, but there are restrictions on formal employment. Running or participating in a business is generally possible. For those who need unrestricted work authorization, the Friendly Nations visa with a labor route, or the Qualified Investor visa, may be more suitable depending on the situation."
    }
  ]}
>
  {/* [VERIFICAR] Pending, NOT published (no figure on the site yet): government fees, attorney fees, translation/apostille costs, medical exam cost.
      [VERIFICAR] Already-published claims worth re-confirming: discount percentages, "visit Panama once every 2 years", "ability to work with Migración authorization",
      Special Passport "140+ countries incl. Schengen/UK" (same claim flagged on the Qualified Investor page), $750 + $100,000 alternative threshold, dependents rule (+$250). */}
  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-slate mb-4">Key figures at a glance</h2>
    <dl class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-sm">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border border-jade/10"><dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-wide">Pension (single)</dt><dd class="text-slate mt-1">$1,000 USD/month, guaranteed for life</dd></div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border border-jade/10"><dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-wide">Pension (couple)</dt><dd class="text-slate mt-1">$1,250/month combined</dd></div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border border-jade/10"><dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-wide">Each additional dependent</dt><dd class="text-slate mt-1">+$250/month above the applicable threshold</dd></div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border border-jade/10"><dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-wide">Alternative route</dt><dd class="text-slate mt-1">$750/month pension + real estate of $100,000 USD or more</dd></div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border border-jade/10"><dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-wide">Typical processing time</dt><dd class="text-slate mt-1">Approx. 6 months</dd></div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-white border border-jade/10"><dt class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-wide">Residency</dt><dd class="text-slate mt-1">Permanent — no renewals · visit Panama at least once every 2 years</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-slate mb-4">Timeline, month by month</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-mid text-sm">
      <li>Month 1: gather documents and obtain apostilles in your home country.</li>
      <li>Month 2: travel to Panama for medical exams and formal submission to the Servicio Nacional de Migración.</li>
      <li>Months 3–5: government review, background checks and inter-ministerial audits.</li>
      <li>Month 6: approval, permanent resident card (carné) and option to apply for a cédula.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-slate mb-4">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-mid text-sm">
      <li>Presenting a pension that is not guaranteed for life — savings, dividends, rental or remote-work income do not qualify.</li>
      <li>Using a criminal background check older than 6 months, or foreign documents that are not apostilled / authenticated.</li>
      <li>Submitting the pension letter without an apostille and a translation by an Authorized Public Translator in Panama.</li>
      <li>Assuming $750/month is enough without the $100,000+ real estate purchase.</li>
      <li>Forgetting the dependents' extra income requirement when applying as a family.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="mt-10 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-fog border border-jade/15">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Compare with other routes</p>
    <div class="space-y-2">
      <a href="/en/visas/comparison/" class="flex items-center gap-2 text-jade text-sm hover:underline"><span>→</span><span>Compare all residency visas side by side</span></a>
      <a href="/en/visas/friendly-nations/" class="flex items-center gap-2 text-jade text-sm hover:underline"><span>→</span><span>Friendly Nations Visa — if you do not have a lifetime pension</span></a>
      <a href="/en/visas/qualified-investor/" class="flex items-center gap-2 text-jade text-sm hover:underline"><span>→</span><span>Qualified Investor Visa — permanent residency by investment</span></a>
      <a href="/en/blog/why-panama-attracts-retirees/" class="flex items-center gap-2 text-jade text-sm hover:underline"><span>→</span><span>Why Panama keeps attracting retirees</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="mt-6 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-fog border border-jade/15">
    <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-mid uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Related guides</p>
    <div class="space-y-2">
      <a href="/en/healthcare/" class="flex items-center gap-2 text-jade text-sm hover:underline">
        <span>→</span>
        <span>Healthcare costs and insurance options in Panama</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/en/pensionado-benefits/" class="flex items-center gap-2 text-jade text-sm hover:underline">
        <span>→</span>
        <span>Full Pensionado discount catalog — all 10 benefit categories</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</VisaPage>
